{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789405848,"nonce":"0gg2zMM3uZhNami3aKYck0OxnR0=","title":"Python: keyed sort, min and max through the typed closure entry and typed keys","message":"`xs.sort(key=lambda p: p[0])`, `sorted(rows, key=...)`, `max(...,\nkey=...)` compute the keys with `zb_list_map1` through the dynamic call\nadapter (`zb_call_1`, a box per argument and per result) into a\n`List\u003cAny\u003e`, and compare them with `zb_any_lt`: about 600 ns per\ncomparison, so 40,000 keyed tuples sort in 0.19 s where the unkeyed\n200,000 ints take 0.03 s.\n\nFix: when the key is a lambda or def whose typed entry inference has\nsettled (`Ty::Closure`), call `name$typed` directly per element into a\nparallel typed key list of the result kind, and sort with the kind's\nown comparison; `reverse=True` chooses the comparison, not a flag in\nthe loop. Depends on the typed-entry work in f1b2928 being reachable\nfrom the library's sort, which takes the key function as a value\ntoday.","files":null}]}